# Command dwebp

## What does this command do:

Decodes and decompresses a .webp image file into PNG, PPM, PGM, BMP, or TIFF.

## Installed from

For Fedora install using: sudo dnf install libwebp-tools
For Raspberry Pi install using: sudo apt install webp
For Mac install using: brew install webp

## Command path

For Fedora run: /usr/bin/dwebp
For Raspberry Pi run: /usr/bin/dwebp
For Mac run: /opt/homebrew/bin/dwebp

## Command documentation

Docs can be found at https://developers.google.com/speed/webp/docs/dwebp

## Command options

Usage: dwebp in_file [options] [-o out_file]

Decodes the WebP image file to PNG format by default. Animated WebP files are not supported.

Output format options:
  -pam ......... save the raw RGBA samples as a color PAM
  -ppm ......... save the raw RGB samples as a color PPM
  -bmp ......... save as uncompressed BMP format
  -tiff ........ save as uncompressed TIFF format
  -pgm ......... save the raw YUV samples as a grayscale PGM (IMC4 layout)
  -yuv ......... save the raw YUV samples in flat layout

Other options:
  -version ..... print version number and exit
  -nofancy ..... don't use the fancy YUV420 upscaler
  -nofilter .... disable in-loop filtering
  -nodither .... disable dithering
  -dither <d> .. dithering strength (in 0..100)
  -alpha_dither  use alpha-plane dithering if needed
  -mt .......... use multi-threading
  -crop <x> <y> <w> <h> ... crop output with the given rectangle
  -resize <w> <h> ......... resize output (*after* any cropping)
  -flip ........ flip the output vertically
  -alpha ....... only save the alpha plane
  -incremental . use incremental decoding (useful for tests)
  -h ........... this help message
  -v ........... verbose (e.g. print decoding times)
  -quiet ....... quiet mode, don't print anything
  -noasm ....... disable all assembly optimizations

Examples:
  # Decode to PNG (default)
  dwebp photo.webp -o photo.png

  # Decode to BMP
  dwebp photo.webp -bmp -o photo.bmp

  # Decode, crop a 100x100 region at (10,10), then resize to 64x64
  dwebp photo.webp -crop 10 10 100 100 -resize 64 64 -o thumb.png
